['```repl\nanswer["content"] = content\nanswer["ready"] = True\nprint("Answer ready, length:", len(answer["content"]))\n```', '# PHASE 2: LIFECYCLE -- Implementation Plan\n\n## 1. Save Points in `run_loop()`\n\n### Location in the Iteration Loop\n\nThe save point is inserted in the `run_loop()` iteration **after** `env.execute()` results are appended to the turn messages, **after** checking `answer.ready`, but **before** the next provider/stream request. This is the only natural safe boundary: the assistant response has been fully processed, all tool results are committed, and no provider request is in flight.\n\n```rust\n// Pseudo-code for save point placement in run_loop()\nloop {\n    // 1. Send turn message -> stream root assistant response\n    let response = stream_provider_request(&session, &config).await;\n\n    // 2. Extract repl blocks from response\n    let blocks = extract_repl_blocks(&response);\n\n    // 3. Execute blocks in env\n    let results = env.execute(&blocks).await;\n\n    // 4. Append results to turn messages\n    turn_state.append_results(&results);\n\n    // 5. Check termination\n    if answer.ready {\n        return StopCause::AnswerReady;\n    }\n\n    // ======= SAVE POINT =======\n    save_point(&mut turn_state, &session, &config, &queues).await;\n    // ======= END SAVE POINT =======\n\n    // 6. Loop detection check\n    if let Some(stop) = loop_detector.check(&blocks, &results) {\n        return stop;\n    }\n\n    // 7. Check guardrails (budget, timeout, tokens, max_iterations)\n    if let Some(stop) = check_guardrails(&run_state) {\n        return stop;\n    }\n    // -> next iteration sends a new provider request\n}\n```\n\n### What State Is Reconciled at the Save Point\n\nThe `save_point()` function performs three operations:\n\n1. **Flush pending session writes** -- any buffered messages, tool results, or state mutations that have been accumulated during this iteration are flushed to the session store. This ensures the session log is durable before the next provider request.\n\n2. **Create a fresh turn snapshot** -- if the loop may continue (i.e., `answer.ready` is false and no guardrail has triggered), a new `TurnState` snapshot is created. This snapshot captures the current message history, accumulated token counts, and iteration count. The snapshot is immutable for the duration of the next iteration.\n\n3. **Apply fresh config** -- the latest config is read and applied to `TurnState` before the next provider request. This includes:\n   - Context window settings\n   - Model selection\n   - Thinking budget / reasoning effort\n   - Stream options\n   - Session ID\n\nThis lets mid-run config changes take effect on the next iteration without restarting the run.\n\n```rust\nasync fn save_point(\n    turn_state: &mut TurnState,\n    session: &Session,\n    config: &Arc<RwLock<PhiConfig>>,\n    queues: &QueueHandles,\n) {\n    // 1. Flush pending session writes\n    session.flush().await;\n\n    // 2. Create fresh turn snapshot if loop may continue\n    turn_state.snapshot();\n\n    // 3. Apply fresh config before next provider request\n    let fresh = config.read().await.clone();\n    turn_state.apply_config(&fresh);\n\n    // 4. Drain queues (see Section 2)\n    drain_queues(turn_state, queues).await;\n}\n```\n\n### Interaction with TurnState\n\n`TurnState` is the per-iteration mutable state that tracks:\n\n```rust\nstruct TurnState {\n    /// Messages accumulated for this turn (incl. tool results)\n    messages: Vec<Message>,\n    /// Immutable snapshot of config at save-point time\n    config_snapshot: PhiConfig,\n    /// Iteration counter\n    iteration: usize,\n    /// Running token count for budget tracking\n    tokens_used: usize,\n    /// Turn snapshot timestamp\n    snapshot_at: Instant,\n}\n```\n\nThe key distinction: **config is snapshotted** (frozen for the next iteration\'s provider request), while **queue contents are live** (drained at each save point). `TurnState::snapshot()` freezes the current config into `config_snapshot`, ensuring the next provider request uses a consistent config even if someone mutates `PhiConfig` concurrently mid-request.\n\n\n## 2. `SteerQueue`, `FollowUpQueue`, `NextTurnQueue`\n\n### Rust Type Definitions\n\nEach queue is a simple FIFO wrapped in `Arc<Mutex<...>>` for thread-safe access from the harness API (which may be called from another thread while `run_loop()` executes).\n\n```rust\nuse std::collections::VecDeque;\nuse std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};\n\n/// Guidance messages injected mid-run. Drained at each save point\n/// and inserted into the next provider request as system/user messages.\nstruct SteerQueue(Arc<Mutex<VecDeque<String>>>);\n\n/// User messages queued for processing after the current run completes.\n/// Drained when run_loop() returns and a new run is about to start.\nstruct FollowUpQueue(Arc<Mutex<VecDeque<String>>>);\n\n/// Messages inserted before the next user-initiated prompt.\n/// Persists across runs; not cleared by abort.\nstruct NextTurnQueue(Arc<Mutex<VecDeque<Message>>>);\n\nstruct QueueHandles {\n    steer: SteerQueue,\n    follow_up: FollowUpQueue,\n    next_turn: NextTurnQueue,\n}\n```\n\n### Queue Semantics\n\n| Queue | Stores | When Drained | Cleared by Abort |\n|-------|--------|--------------|------------------|\n| `SteerQueue` | Guidance strings (mid-run course corrections) | Every save point | Yes |\n| `FollowUpQueue` | User messages (next conversation turn after current run) | When `run_loop()` returns, before starting next run | Yes |\n| `NextTurnQueue` | `Message` values prepended before next user prompt | When a new user-initiated prompt arrives | No |\n\n### Drain Order at Save Points\n\nAt each save point, `drain_queues()` processes queues in this order:\n\n1. **SteerQueue** -- drained fully; each string is appended to `turn_state.messages` as a system or user message. These take effect on the *next* provider request in the same run.\n2. **FollowUpQueue** -- *not* drained at save points. It is only drained when `run_loop()` has returned and a new run is about to begin. Follow-up messages become the user prompt for the next run.\n3. **NextTurnQueue** -- *not* drained at save points. It is drained when the next user-initiated prompt arrives, prepending its messages before that prompt.\n\n```rust\nasync fn drain_queues(turn_state: &mut TurnState, queues: &QueueHandles) {\n    // 1. Drain steer queue — inject into current turn\n    let steer_msgs: Vec<String> = {\n        let mut q = queues.steer.0.lock().unwrap();\n        q.drain(..).collect()\n    };\n    for msg in steer_msgs {\n        turn_state.messages.push(Message::system(msg));\n    }\n    // FollowUp and NextTurn are NOT drained here\n}\n```\n\n### What "Safe Point" Means in Phi\'s Loop\n\nA **safe point** in Phi is the moment between iterations where:\n- The previous assistant response has been fully streamed and processed\n- All repl blocks have been executed and their results appended\n- No provider request is in flight\n- `answer.ready` has been checked (if true, no save point needed -- run terminates)\n\nAt this point, the harness can safely reconcile config, flush session, drain queues, and check loop detection without racing with active I/O.\n\n### Queue Modes: Live vs. Idle\n\n- **Live (during run)**: `steer()`, `follow_up()`, `next_turn()`, and `abort()` are allowed. They enqueue into the `Arc<Mutex<...>>` queues; the run loop drains them at the next safe point.\n- **Idle (between runs)**: Structural operations like `prompt()`, `compact()`, `navigate_tree()` require the engine to be idle (no `run_loop()` active). Attempting these during a run returns `Error::EngineBusy`.\n\n### Why Queue Contents Are Live (Not Snapshotted)\n\nConfig is snapshotted at save points so the next provider request uses a frozen, consistent configuration. Queues, by contrast, are **live** because they represent *new information* arriving from the user or harness *during* the run. If queues were snapshotted, a `steer()` call issued after the save point would be invisible until the next save point -- potentially an entire iteration of stale behavior. By keeping queues live and draining at save points, we ensure the most recent guidance is always incorporated at the next opportunity.\n\n\n## 3. `steer()`, `follow_up()`, `next_turn()` Public API on `PhiHarness`\n\n### Method Signatures and Behavior\n\n```rust\nimpl PhiHarness {\n    /// Inject guidance mid-run. Allowed during a turn (live).\n    /// The message is enqueued into SteerQueue and drained at the next save point,\n    /// where it becomes a system message in the next provider request.\n    pub fn steer(&self, msg: impl Into<String>) -> Result<()> {\n        let mut q = self.queues.steer.0.lock().unwrap();\n        q.push_back(msg.into());\n        Ok(())\n    }\n\n    /// Queue a user message to process after the current run completes.\n    /// Allowed during a turn (live). Drained when run_loop() returns\n    /// and a new run is about to start — becomes the user prompt for that run.\n    pub fn follow_up(&self, msg: impl Into<String>) -> Result<()> {\n        let mut q = self.queues.follow_up.0.lock().unwrap();\n        q.push_back(msg.into());\n        Ok(())\n    }\n\n    /// Insert messages before the next user-initiated prompt.\n    /// Allowed during a turn (live). Persists across runs; not cleared by abort.\n    /// Drained when the next user prompt arrives — prepended to it.\n    pub fn next_turn(&self, msg: Message) -> Result<()> {\n        let mut q = self.queues.next_turn.0.lock().unwrap();\n        q.push_back(msg);\n        Ok(())\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### What Each Method Enqueues\n\n| Method | Queue | Payload | Takes Effect |\n|--------|-------|---------|--------------|\n| `steer()` | `SteerQueue` | `String` (guidance text) | Next save point in current run — injected as system message |\n| `follow_up()` | `FollowUpQueue` | `String` (user message text) | When `run_loop()` returns — becomes prompt for next run |\n| `next_turn()` | `NextTurnQueue` | `Message` (full structured message) | When next user-initiated prompt arrives — prepended |\n\n### When Each Is Allowed\n\nPer Pi\'s model:\n\n- **Queue ops (live):** `steer()`, `follow_up()`, `next_turn()`, `abort()` — allowed **during** a turn. These are non-blocking; they lock the queue mutex briefly, enqueue, and return immediately.\n- **Structural ops (idle only):** `prompt()`, `compact()`, `navigate_tree()` — require the engine to be **idle** (no `run_loop()` active). These mutate session structure and must not race with the run loop.\n\n```rust\n/// Guard for structural ops — returns error if engine is running\nfn require_idle(&self) -> Result<()> {\n    let state = self.run_state.0.lock().unwrap();\n    if state.is_running {\n        Err(Error::EngineBusy)\n    } else {\n        Ok(())\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Thread Safety\n\nAll three queue methods are designed to be called from a **different thread** while `run_loop()` executes on the engine thread:\n\n- The queues use `Arc<Mutex<VecDeque<T>>>`, so the caller gets shared ownership via `Arc<PhiHarness>` and briefly acquires the mutex to enqueue.\n- The lock is held only for the `push_back` operation (microseconds), so contention with the run loop\'s drain at save points is negligible.\n- `Arc<PhiHarness>` can be cloned and handed to a UI thread, background task, or signal handler.\n\n### Interaction with Save-Point Drain\n\nThe `run_loop()` calls `drain_queues()` at each save point (see Section 2). The flow is:\n\n1. External caller invokes `harness.steer("Use a different approach")` from a UI thread.\n2. The string is pushed onto `SteerQueue`.\n3. At the next save point, `drain_queues()` pops the string and pushes it as a `Message::system(...)` into `turn_state.messages`.\n4. The next provider request includes this system message, steering the assistant.\n\n### Error Handling\n\n- Queue methods return `Result<()>` and can only fail if the mutex is poisoned (a previous lock holder panicked). In that case they return `Err(Error::LockPoisoned)`.\n- They do **not** fail if the engine is busy — that is by design for live queue ops.\n\n\n## 4. Loop Detection\n\n### `LoopDetector` Struct\n\nInspired by Crush\'s `hasRepeatedToolCalls(steps, windowSize=10, maxRepeats=5)`, the `LoopDetector` maintains a sliding window of tool-call signatures and detects when any single signature repeats more than the threshold.\n\n```rust\nuse sha2::{Sha256, Digest};\nuse std::collections::VecDeque;\n\nstruct LoopDetector {\n    /// Sliding window of signatures (most recent first).\n    /// Defaults to window_size = 10.\n    window: VecDeque<String>,\n    window_size: usize,\n    /// Max repetitions of the same signature before triggering.\n    /// Defaults to max_repeats = 5.\n    max_repeats: usize,\n}\n\nimpl LoopDetector {\n    fn new(window_size: usize, max_repeats: usize) -> Self {\n        Self {\n            window: VecDeque::with_capacity(window_size),\n            window_size,\n            max_repeats,\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Hash a tool-call signature: SHA-256 of `tool_name\\x00input\\x00result`.\n    /// Only the hex digest is stored in the window to bound memory.\n    fn hash_signature(tool_name: &str, input: &str, result: &str) -> String {\n        let mut hasher = Sha256::new();\n        hasher.update(tool_name.as_bytes());\n        hasher.update(b"\\x00");\n        hasher.update(input.as_bytes());\n        hasher.update(b"\\x00");\n        hasher.update(result.as_bytes());\n        format!("{:x}", hasher.finalize())\n    }\n\n    /// Record a tool call and check if it exceeds the repeat threshold.\n    /// Returns Some(StopCause::LoopDetected) if loop detected, None otherwise.\n    fn check(&mut self, tool_name: &str, input: &str, result: &str) -> Option<StopCause> {\n        let sig = Self::hash_signature(tool_name, input, result);\n\n        // Count occurrences of this signature in the current window\n        let repeats = self.window.iter().filter(|s| **s == sig).count();\n\n        // Push new signature, evict oldest if window is full\n        if self.window.len() >= self.window_size {\n            self.window.pop_front();\n        }\n        self.window.push_back(sig.clone());\n\n        // The new push makes the count repeats + 1\n        if repeats + 1 > self.max_repeats {\n            return Some(StopCause::LoopDetected {\n                signature: sig,\n                repeats: repeats + 1,\n            });\n        }\n        None\n    }\n\n    fn reset(&mut self) {\n        self.window.clear();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### New `StopCause` Variant\n\n```rust\nenum StopCause {\n    AnswerReady,\n    MaxIterations,\n    MaxErrors { count: usize },\n    BudgetExhausted,\n    Timeout,\n    TokenLimit,\n    Cancelled,\n    /// New: loop detected by LoopDetector\n    LoopDetected { signature: String, repeats: usize },\n}\n```\n\n### How It Hashes Tool Calls\n\nThe detector hashes **tool-call signatures** using the same approach as Crush: `tool_name + input + result`. In Phi\'s architecture, these correspond to `RlmEvent::ToolStart` / `RlmEvent::ToolEnd` pairs:\n\n- **ToolStart** provides `tool_name` and `input` (the repl block content or code string).\n- **ToolEnd** provides `result` (the execution output).\n- The signature is `SHA-256(concat(tool_name, "\\x00", input, "\\x00", result))`, stored as a hex digest.\n- Null bytes (`\\x00`) are used as delimiters to prevent ambiguity from concatenation.\n\n### Default Parameters\n\n| Parameter | Default | Purpose |\n|----------|---------|---------|\n| `window_size` | 10 | Number of recent tool-call signatures to track in the sliding window |\n| `max_repeats` | 5 | Maximum allowed repetitions of the same signature before loop is detected |\n\nBoth are configurable via `PhiConfig`:\n\n```rust\nstruct PhiConfig {\n    // ... existing fields ...\n    loop_detection_window: usize, // default: 10\n    loop_detection_max_repeats: usize, // default: 5\n}\n```\n\n### Where in `run_loop()` the Detector Is Called\n\nThe detector is called **after ToolEnd** (i.e., after `env.execute()` returns results and they are appended), **before the save point**:\n\n```rust\n// After results are appended to turn_state\nfor (block, result) in blocks.iter().zip(results.iter()) {\n    let tool_name = block.tool_name(); // e.g., "repl", "bash", etc.\n    let input = block.code();\n    let output = result.to_string();\n    if let Some(stop) = loop_detector.check(tool_name, input, &output) {\n        return stop; // StopCause::LoopDetected\n    }\n}\n// ======= SAVE POINT ======= (only reached if no loop detected)\n```\n\n### Integration with Existing Guardrails\n\nLoop detection is an **additional guardrail**, checked alongside the existing mechanisms:\n\n1. **Loop detection** (new) — checked after each tool execution, before save point\n2. **`max_errors`** (existing) — checked per-iteration; fires on consecutive errors\n3. **`max_iterations`** (existing) — checked per-iteration; fires at 30 iterations\n4. **Budget/timeout/token guardrails** (existing) — checked at save point\n\nThe order of checks in `run_loop()` is:\n\n```rust\n// 1. Loop detection (per tool call, after ToolEnd)\n// 2. answer.ready check (after all blocks processed)\n// 3. Save point (flush, snapshot, config, drain queues)\n// 4. Guardrail checks (max_iterations, budget, timeout, tokens)\n// 5. Next iteration\n```\n\nLoop detection is checked **first** because it is the cheapest check and the most likely to catch wasteful iterations early. When `StopCause::LoopDetected` is returned, the run terminates immediately — the save point is **not** reached, and queues are **not** drained (the harness caller can inspect queue state if needed).\n\n\n## 5. Abort/Cancellation Interaction with Queues\n\n### Per Pi: Abort Clears Steer + FollowUp but NOT NextTurn\n\nWhen an abort/cancellation is triggered, the cleanup semantics are:\n\n- **SteerQueue** — **cleared**. Steer messages are mid-run guidance; if the run is aborted, that guidance is no longer relevant.\n- **FollowUpQueue** — **cleared**. Follow-up messages were intended as the next prompt after the current run. If the user aborts, they are implicitly retracting the continuation.\n- **NextTurnQueue** — **preserved**. Next-turn messages are prepended to the *next user-initiated* prompt, regardless of how the current run ended. They represent context that should survive abort — e.g., system messages, navigation state, or queued structural changes for the next session.\n\n### How `CancellationToken` Triggers the Break in `run_loop()`\n\nPhi\'s existing `RunState` holds an `Arc<Mutex<(CancellationToken, Instant)>>`. The cancel token is checked at two points in the loop:\n\n1. **At the save point** (between iterations) — a lightweight check\n2. **During async awaits** (streaming, env.execute) — the token is `select!`ed against async operations so the loop can break mid-operation\n\n```rust\nloop {\n    // Stream provider response with cancellation\n    let response = tokio::select! {\n        r = stream_provider_request(&session, &config) => r,\n        _ = run_state.cancel_token().cancelled() => {\n            return StopCause::Cancelled;\n        }\n    };\n\n    let blocks = extract_repl_blocks(&response);\n\n    // Execute blocks with cancellation\n    let results = tokio::select! {\n        r = env.execute(&blocks) => r,\n        _ = run_state.cancel_token().cancelled() => {\n            // In-flight execution interrupted\n            return StopCause::Cancelled;\n        }\n    };\n\n    turn_state.append_results(&results);\n\n    if answer.ready { return StopCause::AnswerReady; }\n\n    // Loop detection check\n    if let Some(stop) = loop_detector.check(...) { return stop; }\n\n    // Save point\n    save_point(&mut turn_state, &session, &config, &queues).await;\n\n    // Cancellation check at save point\n    if run_state.cancel_token().is_cancelled() {\n        return StopCause::Cancelled;\n    };\n\n    // Guardrails check\n    if let Some(stop) = check_guardrails(&run_state) { return stop; }\n}\n```\n\n### What Happens to In-Flight Tool Execution on Abort\n\nWhen the `CancellationToken` fires during `env.execute()`:\n\n1. The `tokio::select!` arms, abandoning the `env.execute()` future. The env itself should also drop or cancel child tasks (the `CancellationToken` can be passed into `env` so it can propagate cancellation to child processes).\n2. Partial results from any completed blocks are **lost** — they are not appended to `turn_state` because the `select!` arm returns before `append_results` runs.\n3. The loop returns `StopCause::Cancelled` immediately.\n\n### Abort Flow: Cancel Token -> `run_loop()` Break -> Queue Drain\n\n```rust\nimpl PhiHarness {\n    /// Abort the current run. Allowed during a turn (live).\n    /// Clears steer + followUp queues. Preserves nextTurn queue.\n    pub fn abort(&self) {\n        // 1. Trigger cancellation token\n        {\n            let state = self.run_state.0.lock().unwrap();\n            state.0.cancel(); // CancellationToken::cancel()\n        }\n\n        // 2. Clear steer and follow-up queues (NOT next_turn)\n        self.queues.steer.0.lock().unwrap().clear();\n       .queues.follow_up.0.lock().unwrap().clear();\n        // next_turn is intentionally preserved\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Cleanup at Abort: Which Queues Cleared, Which Preserved, Why\n\n| Queue | Cleared on Abort? | Rationale |\n|-------|-------------------|-----------|\n| `SteerQueue` | Yes | Mid-run guidance is moot once the run is cancelled |\n| `FollowUpQueue` | Yes | Follow-ups were predicated on the current run completing; abort voids that precondition |\n| `NextTurnQueue` | No | Next-turn messages are independent of the current run; they apply to the next user-initiated prompt regardless of how this run ended |\n\n### Interaction with Save Points\n\nAbort can happen at **any point**, not just safe points. The two mechanisms interact as follows:\n\n- **Abort during streaming/execution**: The `tokio::select!` arms, `run_loop()` returns `StopCause::Cancelled`, and the harness\'s `abort()` method clears steer/follow-up queues. The save point is **never reached**.\n- **Abort at save point**: If `cancel_token().is_cancelled()` is true when checked at the save point, the loop returns `StopCause::Cancelled` before draining queues. The harness\'s `abort()` has already cleared steer/follow-up. `NextTurnQueue` is preserved.\n- **Abort after save point, before next iteration**: The cancellation token is checked at the top of the next iteration\'s `tokio::select!`, causing immediate return.\n\n### Post-Abort State\n\nAfter abort completes:\n- `RunState.is_running` is set to `false` (the engine returns to idle)\n- `SteerQueue` is empty\n- `FollowUpQueue` is empty\n- `NextTurnQueue` retains its messages\n- The session log contains all fully-completed iterations up to (but not including) the aborted iteration\n- The caller can immediately start a new run (structural ops are allowed again since the engine is idle)\n', '```repl\n# Write the plan to a file first to avoid string escaping issues\nplan_text = """# Phase 3: Hooks Refactor\n\n## Overview\n\nReplace Phi\'s current string-keyed, un-chained event system (EventKind/EventPayload/EventReturn/EventHandler) with a typed, chained hooks system modeled on Pi\'s AgentHarnessHooks design, translated to Rust\'s trait + TypeId dispatch model.\n\nThe core shift: each event is a concrete struct (not an enum variant), each event type declares its own result type via an associated type, handlers are registered per event type and chained sequentially (each seeing prior output), and the runtime calls a single PhiHooks::emit() entry point.\n\n---\n\n## 1. New HookEvent / ResultOf Trait Pattern in Rust\n\n### Design: trait with associated type\n\nTypeScript uses phantom type parameters (HookEvent<TType, TResult> and ResultOf<E>). Rust\'s equivalent is a trait with an associated type -- each event struct implements the trait and specifies its result type.\n\n```rust\n// phi-ext/src/hooks.rs\n\nuse std::any::TypeId;\nuse std::sync::Arc;\nuse anyhow::Result;\n\n/// Marker type for events that produce no result.\n#[derive(Clone, Debug)]\npub struct VoidResult;\n\n/// Core trait: each event type defines its own result type.\n/// Rust equivalent of TypeScript\'s HookEvent<TType, TResult> + ResultOf<E>.\npub trait HookEvent: Send + Sync + \'static {\n    /// The result type this event produces. Use VoidResult for observation-only events.\n    type Result: Clone + Send + Sync + \'static;\n\n    /// String name for debugging, introspection, and Lua interop.\n    fn event_name() -> &\'static str where Self: Sized;\n\n    /// TypeId for internal dispatch routing.\n    fn type_id(&self) -> TypeId { TypeId::of::<Self>() }\n}\n```\n\nThe associated type `Result` is the Rust analogue of `ResultOf<E>`. Where TypeScript infers `ResultOf<E>` via conditional types, Rust resolves it through the trait impl: `<E as HookEvent>::Result`.\n\n### Concrete event structs\n\nEach event replaces a variant of the old `EventPayload` enum. The event struct carries its own payload fields directly:\n\n```rust\nuse serde_json::Value;\n\n// --- Events with results ---\n\npub struct ContextEvent {\n    pub turn: u32,\n    pub messages: Vec<AgentMessage>,\n}\n\npub struct ContextResult {\n    pub messages: Option<Vec<AgentMessage>>,\n}\n\nimpl HookEvent for ContextEvent {\n    type Result = ContextResult;\n    fn event_name() -> &\'static str { "context" }\n}\n\npub struct ToolCallEvent {\n    pub tool_name: String,\n    pub input: Value,\n}\n\npub struct ToolCallResult {\n    pub block: bool,\n    pub reason: Option<String>,\n}\n\nimpl HookEvent for ToolCallEvent {\n    type Result = ToolCallResult;\n    fn event_name() -> &\'static str { "tool_call" }\n}\n\npub struct ToolResultEvent {\n    pub tool_name: String,\n    pub content: String,\n    pub details: Option<String>,\n    pub is_error: bool,\n}\n\npub struct ToolResultResult {\n    pub content: Option<String>,\n    pub details: Option<String>,\n    pub is_error: Option<bool>,\n}\n\nimpl HookEvent for ToolResultEvent {\n    type Result = ToolResultResult;\n    fn event_name() -> &\'static str { "tool_result" }\n}\n\npub struct BeforeAgentStartEvent {\n    pub prompt: String,\n    pub system_prompt: String,\n}\n\npub struct BeforeAgentStartResult {\n    pub system_prompt: Option<String>,\n    pub messages: Vec<AgentMessage>,\n}\n\nimpl HookEvent for BeforeAgentStartEvent {\n    type Result = BeforeAgentStartResult;\n    fn event_name() -> &\'static str { "before_agent_start" }\n}\n\npub struct InputEvent {\n    pub text: String,\n}\n\npub struct InputResult {\n    pub action: InputAction,\n    pub text: Option<String>,\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone, Debug)]\npub enum InputAction {\n    Continue,\n    Transform,\n    Handled,\n}\n\nimpl HookEvent for InputEvent {\n    type Result = InputResult;\n    fn event_name() -> &\'static str { "input" }\n}\n\n// --- Observation-only events (no result) ---\n\npub struct TurnStartEvent { pub turn: u32 }\nimpl HookEvent for TurnStartEvent {\n    type Result = VoidResult;\n    fn event_name() -> &\'static str { "turn_start" }\n}\n\npub struct TurnEndEvent { pub turn: u32, pub had_code: bool }\nimpl HookEvent for TurnEndEvent {\n    type Result = VoidResult;\n    fn event_name() -> &\'static str { "turn_end" }\n}\n\npub struct AgentStartEvent { pub prompt: String }\nimpl HookEvent for AgentStartEvent {\n    type Result = VoidResult;\n    fn event_name() -> &\'static str { "agent_start" }\n}\n\npub struct AgentEndEvent { pub stop_cause: String }\nimpl HookEvent for AgentEndEvent {\n    type Result = VoidResult;\n    fn event_name() -> &\'static str { "agent_end" }\n}\n\npub struct SessionStartEvent { pub cwd: String, pub session_id: String }\nimpl HookEvent for SessionStartEvent {\n    type Result = VoidResult;\n    fn event_name() -> &\'static str { "session_start" }\n}\n\npub struct RuntimeStartEvent;\nimpl HookEvent for RuntimeStartEvent {\n    type Result = VoidResult;\n    fn event_name() -> &\'static str { "runtime_start" }\n}\n\npub struct SessionShutdownEvent;\nimpl HookEvent for SessionShutdownEvent {\n    type Result = VoidResult;\n    fn event_name() -> &\'static str { "session_shutdown" }\n}\n```\n\n(Assume `AgentMessage` is imported from `phi-ai` or defined as a shared type in `phi-ext`.)\n\n### Trade-offs vs TypeScript phantom types\n\n| Aspect | TypeScript | Rust |\n|--------|-----------|------|\n| Result type binding | Phantom type parameter on interface | Associated type on trait impl |\n| Type-level extraction | `ResultOf<E>` conditional type | `<E as HookEvent>::Result` |\n| Dispatch routing | `Map<string, Set<Handler>>` keyed by type string | `HashMap<TypeId, Vec<HandlerEntry>>` keyed by `TypeId::of::<E>()` |\n| Handler type erasure | `Map<string, Set<HookHandler<any, Ctx>>` with internal casts | `Box<dyn Fn(&dyn Any) -> Result<Box<dyn Any>>>` with downcast |\n| Compile-time safety | Partially lost at storage boundary due to `any` | Partially lost at storage boundary due to `dyn Any`; recovers via downcast in `emit()` |\n\nBoth designs accept type erasure at the storage layer and recover type safety at the `emit()` boundary. Rust\'s `TypeId` + `Any` downcast replaces TypeScript\'s string-keyed `Map<string, ...>`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. PhiHooks Struct\n\n### Handler storage strategy\n\nHandlers are stored in a type-erased form keyed by `TypeId`. The `on()` method captures the generic type parameter at registration time, and `emit()` recovers it via downcast.\n\n```rust\nuse std::any::{Any, TypeId};\nuse std::collections::HashMap;\nuse std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};\nuse anyhow::Result;\n\n// --- Type-erased handler function ---\n// Stored as Box<dyn Fn(&dyn Any) -> Result<Option<Box<dyn Any + Send>>>>\n// The &dyn Any argument is &E; the return is Option<E::Result> boxed.\n\ntype AnyHandler = Arc<dyn Fn(&dyn Any) -> Result<Option<Box<dyn Any + Send>>> + Send + Sync>;\ntype AnyObserver = Arc<dyn Fn(&dyn Any, TypeId) -> Result<()> + Send + Sync>;\ntype CleanupFn = Arc<dyn Fn() -> Result<()> + Send + Sync>;\n\npub struct HandlerEntry {\n    pub handler: AnyHandler,\n    pub source: Option<SourceInfo>,\n}\n\npub struct ObserverEntry {\n    pub observer: AnyObserver,\n    pub source: Option<SourceInfo>,\n}\n\npub struct CleanupEntry {\n    pub cleanup: CleanupFn,\n    pub source: Option<SourceInfo>,\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone, Debug)]\npub struct SourceInfo {\n    pub extension_id: String,\n    pub label: Option<String>,\n}\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug)]\npub enum ErrorMode {\n    Continue,\n    Throw,\n}\n\npub struct PhiHooks {\n    observers: Mutex<Vec<ObserverEntry>>,\n    handlers: Mutex<HashMap<TypeId, Vec<HandlerEntry>>>,\n    cleanups: Mutex<Vec<CleanupEntry>>,\n    error_mode: ErrorMode,\n}\n```\n\n### Method signatures\n\n```rust\nimpl PhiHooks {\n    pub fn new(error_mode: ErrorMode) -> Self {\n        Self {\n            observers: Mutex::new(Vec::new()),\n            handlers: Mutex::new(HashMap::new()),\n            cleanups: Mutex::new(Vec::new()),\n            error_mode,\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Read-only observer: sees all events, return ignored.\n    /// Returns a deregistration closure.\n    pub fn observe<F>(&self, observer: F) -> Box<dyn FnOnce() + Send + Sync>\n    where\n        F: Fn(&dyn Any, TypeId) -> Result<()> + Send + Sync + \'static,\n    {\n        let entry = ObserverEntry {\n            observer: Arc::new(observer),\n            source: None,\n        };\n        let idx = {\n            let mut obs = self.observers.lock().unwrap();\n            obs.push(entry);\n            obs.len() - 1\n        };\n        let observers = Arc::clone(unsafe { &*(&self.observers as *const Mutex<Vec<ObserverEntry>>) });\n        Box::new(move || {\n            let mut obs = observers.lock().unwrap();\n            if idx < obs.len() {\n                obs[idx].observer = Arc::new(|_, _| Ok(()));  // null out\n            }\n        })\n    }\n\n    /// Participating handler: registered for a specific event type E.\n    /// Returns a deregistration closure.\n    pub fn on<E, F>(&self, handler: F) -> Box<dyn FnOnce() + Send + Sync>\n    where\n        E: HookEvent,\n        F: Fn(&E) -> Result<Option<E::Result>> + Send + Sync + \'static,\n    {\n        self.on_with_source::<E, F>(handler, None)\n    }\n\n    /// on() with explicit source metadata.\n    pub fn on_with_source<E, F>(\n        &self,\n        handler: F,\n        source: Option<SourceInfo>,\n    ) -> Box<dyn FnOnce() + Send + Sync>\n    where\n        E: HookEvent,\n        F: Fn(&E) -> Result<Option<E::Result>> + Send + Sync + \'static,\n    {\n        // Erase: wrap typed handler into AnyHandler.\n        let handler_arc = Arc::new(handler);\n        let erased: AnyHandler = Arc::new(move |event_any: &dyn Any| {\n            let event = event_any.downcast_ref::<E>()\n                .ok_or_else(|| anyhow::anyhow!("type mismatch in handler dispatch"))?;\n            let result = handler_arc(event)?;\n            Ok(result.map(|r| Box::new(r) as Box<dyn Any + Send>))\n        });\n\n        let type_id = TypeId::of::<E>();\n        let entry = HandlerEntry { handler: erased, source };\n\n        let idx = {\n            let mut h = self.handlers.lock().unwrap();\n            let vec = h.entry(type_id).or_default();\n            vec.push(entry);\n            vec.len() - 1\n        };\n\n        // Return deregistration closure\n        let handlers = self.handlers_arc();\n        Box::new(move || {\n            let mut h = handlers.lock().unwrap();\n            if let Some(vec) = h.get_mut(&type_id) {\n                if idx < vec.len() {\n                    vec[idx].handler = Arc::new(|_| Ok(None));  // null out\n                }\n            }\n        })\n    }\n\n    /// Single entry point. Harness/runtime only calls this.\n    pub async fn emit<E: HookEvent>(&self, event: E) -> Result<Option<E::Result>> {\n        // 1. Run all observers (read-only, errors logged not thrown)\n        self.run_observers(&event)?;\n\n        // 2. Run handlers with event-specific chaining\n        let result = self.run_handlers_chained(&event).await?;\n\n        Ok(result)\n    }\n\n    /// Register a cleanup function, runs on clear()/dispose().\n    pub fn add_cleanup<F>(&self, cleanup: F) -> Box<dyn FnOnce() + Send + Sync>\n    where\n        F: Fn() -> Result<()> + Send + Sync + \'static,\n    {\n        self.add_cleanup_with_source(cleanup, None)\n    }\n\n    pub fn add_cleanup_with_source<F>(\n        &self,\n        cleanup: F,\n        source: Option<SourceInfo>,\n    ) -> Box<dyn FnOnce() + Send + Sync>\n    where\n        F: Fn() -> Result<()> + Send + Sync + \'static,\n    {\n        let entry = CleanupEntry { cleanup: Arc::new(cleanup), source };\n        let idx = {\n            let mut c = self.cleanups.lock().unwrap();\n            c.push(entry);\n            c.len() - 1\n        };\n        let cleanups = self.cleanups_arc();\n        Box::new(move || {\n            let mut c = cleanups.lock().unwrap();\n            if idx < c.len() {\n                c[idx].cleanup = Arc::new(|| Ok(()));\n            }\n        })\n    }\n\n    /// Remove all handlers/observers, run all cleanups.\n    pub async fn clear(&self) -> Result<()> {\n        let cleanups = {\n            let mut c = self.cleanups.lock().unwrap();\n            std::mem::take(&mut *c)\n        };\n        for entry in cleanups {\n            if let Err(e) = entry.cleanup() {\n                if self.error_mode == ErrorMode::Throw { return Err(e); }\n                eprintln!("[hooks] cleanup error: {e}");\n            }\n        }\n        self.observers.lock().unwrap().clear();\n        self.handlers.lock().unwrap().clear();\n        Ok(())\n    }\n\n    /// Alias for clear() + mark as disposed (no new registrations).\n    pub async fn dispose(&self) -> Result<()> {\n        self.clear().await\n    }\n\n    /// Create a scope with source metadata for all registrations.\n    pub fn create_scope(&self, source: SourceInfo) -> PhiHooksScope<\'_> {\n        PhiHooksScope { hooks: self, source }\n    }\n\n    // --- Internal helpers ---\n\n    fn run_observers<E: HookEvent>(&self, event: &E) -> Result<()> {\n        let type_id = TypeId::of::<E>();\n        let observers = self.observers.lock().unwrap();\n        for entry in observers.iter() {\n            let event_ref: &dyn Any = event;\n            if let Err(e) = (entry.observer)(event_ref, type_id) {\n                match self.error_mode {\n                    ErrorMode::Throw => return Err(e),\n                    ErrorMode::Continue => eprintln!("[hooks] observer error: {e}"),\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        Ok(())\n    }\n\n    async fn run_handlers_chained<E: HookEvent>(&self, event: &E) -> Result<Option<E::Result>> {\n        // Dispatch to the event-specific reducer.\n        // See Section 3 for reducer dispatch.\n        self.dispatch_reducer(event).await\n    }\n\n    // (handlers_arc / cleanups_arc return Arc clones of the Mutex fields)\n}\n```\n\n### PhiHooksScope for source metadata\n\n```rust\npub struct PhiHooksScope<\'a> {\n    hooks: &\'a PhiHooks,\n    source: SourceInfo,\n}\n\nimpl<\'a> PhiHooksScope<\'a> {\n    pub fn on<E, F>(&self, handler: F) -> Box<dyn FnOnce() + Send + Sync>\n    where\n        E: HookEvent,\n        F: Fn(&E) -> Result<Option<E::Result>> + Send + Sync + \'static,\n    {\n        self.hooks.on_with_source::<E, F>(handler, Some(self.source.clone()))\n    }\n\n    pub fn observe<F>(&self, observer: F) -> Box<dyn FnOnce() + Send + Sync>\n    where\n        F: Fn(&dyn Any, TypeId) -> Result<()> + Send + Sync + \'static,\n    {\n        //observe_with_source would be analogous\n        self.hooks.observe(observer)  // simplified; full version tags source\n    }\n\n    pub fn add_cleanup<F>(&self, cleanup: F) -> Box<dyn FnOnce() + Send + Sync>\n    where\n        F: Fn() -> Result<()> + Send + Sync + \'static,\n    {\n        self.hooks.add_cleanup_with_source(cleanup, Some(self.source.clone()))\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Result Chaining Reducers\n\n### Reducer dispatch\n\n`emit()` delegates to `dispatch_reducer()` which matches on `TypeId` to select the correct chaining strategy. This is a single match inside `PhiHooks` rather than a separate `Reducer` trait, because the chaining logic is inherent to each event type\'s semantics.\n\nHowever, for extensibility, a `Reducer` trait can be defined so that custom event types can plug in their own chaining:\n\n```rust\n#[async_trait::async_trait]\npub trait Reducer<E: HookEvent>: Send + Sync {\n    async fn reduce(\n        &self,\n        event: &E,\n        handlers: &[HandlerEntry],\n        error_mode: ErrorMode,\n    ) -> Result<Option<E::Result>>;\n}\n```\n\nIn practice, the built-in reducers are matched inside `dispatch_reducer()`:\n\n```rust\nimpl PhiHooks {\n    async fn dispatch_reducer<E: HookEvent>(&self, event: &E) -> Result<Option<E::Result>> {\n        let type_id = TypeId::of::<E>();\n\n        // Observation-only events: run handlers, ignore results.\n        if type_id == TypeId::of::<RuntimeStartEvent>()\n            || type_id == TypeId::of::<SessionStartEvent>()\n            || type_id == TypeId::of::<TurnStartEvent>()\n            || type_id == TypeId::of::<TurnEndEvent>()\n            || type_id == TypeId::of::<AgentStartEvent>()\n            || type_id == TypeId::of::<AgentEndEvent>()\n            || type_id == TypeId::of::<SessionShutdownEvent>()\n        {\n            return self.reduce_observation(event).await;\n        }\n\n        if type_id == TypeId::of::<ContextEvent>() {\n            return self.reduce_context_any(event).await;\n        }\n\n        if type_id == TypeId::of::<ToolCallEvent>() {\n            return self.reduce_tool_call_any(event).await;\n        }\n\n        if type_id == TypeId::of::<ToolResultEvent>() {\n            return self.reduce_tool_result_any(event).await;\n        }\n\n        if type_id == TypeId::of::<BeforeAgentStartEvent>() {\n            return self.reduce_before_agent_start_any(event).await;\n        }\n\n        if type_id == TypeId::of::<InputEvent>() {\n            return self.reduce_input_any(event).await;\n        }\n\n        // Default: observation-only\n        self.reduce_observation(event).await\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Concrete reducer: context transform\n\nSequential, each handler sees current messages:\n\n```rust\nimpl PhiHooks {\n    async fn reduce_context(&self, event: &ContextEvent) -> Result<Option<ContextResult>> {\n        let handlers = self.get_handlers::<ContextEvent>();\n        let mut current_messages = event.messages.clone();\n        let mut modified = false;\n\n        for entry in handlers.iter() {\n            let handler = &entry.handler;\n            let event_any: &dyn Any = event;\n\n            // But we need to pass current_messages, not original event.\n            // So we construct a modified event for each handler:\n            let current_event = ContextEvent {\n                turn: event.turn,\n                messages: current_messages.clone(),\n            };\n            let event_ref: &dyn Any = &current_event;\n\n            let result = match handler(event_ref) {\n                Ok(r) => r,\n                Err(e) => return Err(self.handle_error(e, &entry.source)),\n            };\n\n            if let Some(boxed) = result {\n                let r = boxed.downcast_ref::<ContextResult>()\n                    .ok_or_else(|| anyhow::anyhow!("type mismatch in context result"))?;\n                if let Some(ref msgs) = r.messages {\n                    current_messages = msgs.clone();\n                    modified = true;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        if modified {\n            Ok(Some(ContextResult { messages: Some(current_messages) }))\n        } else {\n            Ok(None)\n        }\n    }\n\n    fn handle_error(&self, e: anyhow::Error, source: &Option<SourceInfo>) -> anyhow::Error {\n        let src = source.as_ref()\n            .map(|s| s.extension_id.as_str())\n            .unwrap_or("unknown");\n        eprintln!("[hooks] handler error ({}): {}", src, e);\n        match self.error_mode {\n            ErrorMode::Throw => e,\n            ErrorMode::Continue => anyhow::anyhow!("swallowed: {}", e),  // caller checks mode\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Concrete reducer: tool_call (early exit on block)\n\n```rust\nimpl PhiHooks {\n    async fn reduce_tool_call(&self, event: &ToolCallEvent) -> Result<Option<ToolCallResult>> {\n        let handlers = self.get_handlers::<ToolCallEvent>();\n\n        for entry in handlers.iter() {\n            let event_any: &dyn Any = event;\n            let result = match entry.handler(event_any) {\n                Ok(r) => r,\n                Err(e) => {\n                    if self.error_mode == ErrorMode::Throw { return Err(e); }\n                    eprintln!("[hooks] tool_call handler error: {e}");\n                    continue;\n                }\n            };\n\n            if let Some(boxed) = result {\n                let r = boxed.downcast_ref::<ToolCallResult>()\n                    .ok_or_else(|| anyhow::anyhow!("type mismatch in tool_call result"))?;\n                if r.block {\n                    return Ok(Some(ToolCallResult {\n                        block: true,\n                        reason: r.reason.clone(),\n                    }));\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        Ok(None)  // No block\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Concrete reducer: tool_result (patch accumulation)\n\n```rust\nimpl PhiHooks {\n    async fn reduce_tool_result(&self, event: &ToolResultEvent) -> Result<Option<ToolResultResult>> {\n        let handlers = self.get_handlers::<ToolResultEvent>();\n        let mut content = event.content.clone();\n        let mut details = event.details.clone();\n        let mut is_error = event.is_error;\n        let mut modified = false;\n\n        for entry in handlers.iter() {\n            // Construct current event for this handler\n            let current_event = ToolResultEvent {\n                tool_name: event.tool_name.clone(),\n                content: content.clone(),\n                details: details.clone(),\n                is_error,\n            };\n            let event_ref: &dyn Any = &current_event;\n\n            let result = match entry.handler(event_ref) {\n                Ok(r) => r,\n                Err(e) => {\n                    if self.error_mode == ErrorMode::Throw { return Err(e); }\n                    continue;\n                }\n            };\n\n            if let Some(boxed) = result {\n                let r = boxed.downcast_ref::<ToolResultResult>()\n                    .ok_or_else(|| anyhow::anyhow!("type mismatch"))?;\n                if let Some(ref c) = r.content { content = c.clone(); modified = true; }\n                if let Some(ref d) = r.details { details = Some(d.clone()); modified = true; }\n                if let Some(ie) = r.is_error { is_error = ie; modified = true; }\n            }\n        }\n\n        if modified {\n            Ok(Some(ToolResultResult {\n                content: Some(content),\n                details,\n                is_error: Some(is_error),\n            }))\n        } else {\n            Ok(None)\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Concrete reducer: observation-only (no result)\n\n```rust\nimpl PhiHooks {\n    async fn reduce_observation<E: HookEvent>(&self, event: &E) -> Result<Option<E::Result>> {\n        let handlers = self.get_handlers::<E>();\n        for entry in handlers.iter() {\n            let event_any: &dyn Any = event;\n            if let Err(e) = entry.handler(event_any) {\n                if self.error_mode == ErrorMode::Throw { return Err(e); }\n                eprintln!("[hooks] handler error: {e}");\n            }\n        }\n        Ok(None)  // No result for observation-only events\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Concrete reducer: BeforeAgentStart (collect messages + chain system prompt)\n\n```rust\nimpl PhiHooks {\n    async fn reduce_before_agent_start(\n        &self,\n        event: &BeforeAgentStartEvent,\n    ) -> Result<Option<BeforeAgentStartResult>> {\n        let handlers = self.get_handlers::<BeforeAgentStartEvent>();\n        let mut system_prompt = event.system_prompt.clone();\n        let mut messages = Vec::new();\n\n        for entry in handlers.iter() {\n            let current_event = BeforeAgentStartEvent {\n                prompt: event.prompt.clone(),\n                system_prompt: system_prompt.clone(),\n            };\n            let event_ref: &dyn Any = &current_event;\n\n            let result = match entry.handler(event_ref) {\n                Ok(r) => r,\n                Err(e) => {\n                    if self.error_mode == ErrorMode::Throw { return Err(e); }\n                    continue;\n                }\n            };\n\n            if let Some(boxed) = result {\n                let r = boxed.downcast_ref::<BeforeAgentStartResult>()\n                    .ok_or_else(|| anyhow::anyhow!("type mismatch"))?;\n                if let Some(ref sp) = r.system_prompt { system_prompt = sp.clone(); }\n                messages.extend(r.messages.iter().cloned());\n            }\n        }\n\n        let changed = !messages.is_empty() || system_prompt != event.system_prompt;\n        if changed {\n            Ok(Some(BeforeAgentStartResult {\n                system_prompt: Some(system_prompt),\n                messages,\n            }))\n        } else {\n            Ok(None)\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Concrete reducer: Input (transform chain, handled short-circuits)\n\n```rust\nimpl PhiHooks {\n    async fn reduce_input(&self, event: &InputEvent) -> Result<Option<InputResult>> {\n        let handlers = self.get_handlers::<InputEvent>();\n        let mut text = event.text.clone();\n\n        for entry in handlers.iter() {\n            let current_event = InputEvent { text: text.clone() };\n            let event_ref: &dyn Any = &current_event;\n\n            let result = match entry.handler(event_ref) {\n                Ok(r) => r,\n                Err(e) => {\n                    if self.error_mode == ErrorMode::Throw { return Err(e); }\n                    continue;\n                }\n            };\n\n            if let Some(boxed) = result {\n                let r = boxed.downcast_ref::<InputResult>()\n                    .ok_or_else(|| anyhow::anyhow!("type mismatch"))?;\n                match r.action {\n                    InputAction::Handled => {\n                        return Ok(Some(InputResult {\n                            action: InputAction::Handled,\n                            text: r.text.clone(),\n                        }));\n                    }\n                    InputAction::Transform => {\n                        if let Some(ref t) = r.text { text = t.clone(); }\n                    }\n                    InputAction::Continue => {}\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        if text != event.text {\n            Ok(Some(InputResult {\n                action: InputAction::Transform,\n                text: Some(text),\n            }))\n        } else {\n            Ok(None)\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Summary of reducer strategies\n\n| Event Type | Strategy | Key Behavior |\n|-----------|----------|-------------|\n| Context | Sequential transform | Each handler sees current messages; returns modified messages |\n| BeforeAgentStart | Collect + chain | Accumulates injected messages; chains system_prompt |\n| ToolCall | Early exit | Stops on first block=true |\n| ToolResult | Patch accumulation | Each handler sees current patched content/details/is_error |\n| Input | Transform + short-circuit | Transform chains text; Handled stops immediately |\n| Session-before | Early exit on cancel | First cancel=true wins |\n| Observation-only | Fire-and-forget | Handlers run, results ignored |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Source Metadata / Scopes\n\n### SourceInfo struct\n\n```rust\n#[derive(Clone, Debug)]\npub struct SourceInfo {\n    pub extension_id: String,\n    pub label: Option<String>,\n}\n```\n\n### Scope creation\n\nEvery `HandlerEntry`, `ObserverEntry`, and `CleanupEntry` carries an `Option<SourceInfo>`. The `PhiHooksScope` is a lightweight borrow that auto-tags all registrations:\n\n```rust\n// PhiHooks::create_scope() returns PhiHooksScope (defined in Section 2)\n\n// Usage during extension registration:\nimpl ExtensionHost for RegistryHost {\n    fn register_extension_with_hooks(&mut self, manifest: &ExtensionManifest, hooks: &PhiHooks) -> Result<()> {\n        let scope = hooks.create_scope(SourceInfo {\n            extension_id: manifest.id.clone(),\n            label: Some(manifest.name.clone()),\n        });\n        // Extension gets a PhiHooksScope reference for registering handlers\n        // ...\n        Ok(())\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Source propagation into results\n\nFor events where source attribution matters (e.g., tool_result overrides, message injections), result structs can carry an optional source:\n\n```rust\n/// Wrapper for results that need source attribution.\n#[derive(Clone, Debug)]\npub struct Sourced<T> {\n    pub value: T,\n    pub source: Option<SourceInfo>,\n}\n```\n\nFor `BeforeAgentStartResult`, each injected message can be tagged:\n\n```rust\npub struct BeforeAgentStartResult {\n    pub system_prompt: Option<String>,\n    pub messages: Vec<Sourced<AgentMessage>>,  // each message knows its origin\n}\n```\n\n### Error attribution\n\nWhen a handler returns `Err`, the error is enriched with source metadata in `handle_error()`:\n\n```rust\nfn handle_error(&self, e: anyhow::Error, source: &Option<SourceInfo>) -> anyhow::Error {\n    let src = source.as_ref()\n        .map(|s| format!("{}:{}", s.extension_id, s.label.as_deref().unwrap_or("")))\n        .unwrap_or_else(|| "unknown".to_string());\n    eprintln!("[hooks] error from [{}]: {}", src, e);\n\n    match self.error_mode {\n        ErrorMode::Throw => anyhow::anyhow!("[{}] {}", src, e),\n        ErrorMode::Continue => {\n            // Error logged, swallowed, None returned\n            anyhow::anyhow!("[{}] {}", src, e)\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### on() with inline source\n\nBoth paths are supported: scope-based (auto-tagged) and inline:\n\n```rust\n// Scope-based (preferred for extensions)\nlet scope = hooks.create_scope(SourceInfo { extension_id: "lua:my-ext".into(), label: None });\nscope.on::<ContextEvent, _>(|event| { ... });\n\n// Inline (for ad-hoc registrations from runtime/builtins)\nhooks.on_with_source::<ContextEvent, _>(handler, Some(SourceInfo { extension_id: "builtin".into(), label: None }));\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Migration Path\n\n### Step-by-step ordering\n\n1. **phi-ext: Add new `hooks` module** (additive, no breaking changes)\n2. **phi-runtime: Add `PhiHooks` to `AppRuntime`** (coexist with old `event_handlers`)\n3. **phi-runtime: Wire `dispatch()` to delegate to `emit()`** (new path active, old path as fallback)\n4. **phi-builtins: No event usage currently; nothing to migrate**\n5. **phi-lua: Translate `phi.on()` to typed hook registrations**\n6. **phi-runtime: Remove old `EventKind`/`EventPayload`/`EventReturn`/`EventHandlerRegistration`**\n\n### 5a. phi-ext changes\n\n**New file:** `phi-ext/src/hooks.rs` -- all types from Sections 1-4.\n\n**lib.rs changes:**\n\n```rust\npub mod hooks;\npub use hooks::{\n    HookEvent, VoidResult, PhiHooks, PhiHooksScope, SourceInfo, ErrorMode,\n    HandlerEntry, ObserverEntry, CleanupEntry,\n    // Event types\n    ContextEvent, ContextResult,\n    ToolCallEvent, ToolCallResult,\n    ToolResultEvent, ToolResultResult,\n    BeforeAgentStartEvent, BeforeAgentStartResult,\n    InputEvent, InputResult, InputAction,\n    TurnStartEvent, TurnEndEvent,\n    AgentStartEvent, AgentEndEvent,\n    SessionStartEvent, RuntimeStartEvent, SessionShutdownEvent,\n};\n\n// ExtensionHost trait evolves:\npub trait ExtensionHost {\n    fn register_tool(&mut self, tool: ToolSpec) -> Result<()>;\n    fn register_command(&mut self, command: CommandSpec) -> Result<()>;\n    fn hooks(&self) -> &PhiHooks;  // NEW: extensions register hooks via this\n    // OLD: fn subscribe(&mut self, handler: EventHandlerRegistration) -> Result<()>;\n    // DEPRECATED - remove in step 6\n}\n```\n\nOld types (`EventKind`, `EventPayload`, `EventReturn`, `LifecycleEvent`, `EventHandlerRegistration`, `EventHandler`) are marked `#[deprecated]` in step 1 and removed in step 6.\n\n### 5b. phi-runtime changes\n\n`AppRuntime` gains a `hooks: PhiHooks` field. During `RuntimeBuilder::build()`, each extension\'s `register()` is called with access to `&PhiHooks`:\n\n```rust\npub struct AppRuntime {\n    cwd: PathBuf,\n    session_id: String,\n    extensions: Vec<ExtensionManifest>,\n    tools: BTreeMap<String, ToolSpec>,\n    commands: BTreeMap<String, CommandSpec>,\n    hooks: PhiHooks,                      // NEW\n    event_handlers: Vec<EventHandlerRegistration>,  // DEPRECATED, removed in step 6\n    notifications: Mutex<Vec<String>>,\n    status: Mutex<BTreeMap<String, String>>,\n}\n```\n\n`RegistryHost` implements `ExtensionHost` with the new `hooks()` method:\n\n```rust\nstruct RegistryHost {\n    tools: BTreeMap<String, ToolSpec>,\n    commands: BTreeMap<String, CommandSpec>,\n    hooks: PhiHooks,  // NEW\n    event_handlers: Vec<EventHandlerRegistration>,  // DEPRECATED\n}\n\nimpl ExtensionHost for RegistryHost {\n    fn register_tool(&mut self, tool: ToolSpec) -> Result<()> { /* unchanged */ }\n    fn register_command(&mut self, command: CommandSpec) -> Result<()> { /* unchanged */ }\n    fn hooks(&self) -> &PhiHooks { &self.hooks }\n    // OLD subscribe() still present during migration\n}\n```\n\n### 5c. phi-builtins changes\n\nMinimal. `CoreMetaExtension` does not subscribe to any events currently. No migration needed. If builtins want to observe events in the future, they use `host.hooks().on::<Event, _>(handler)` or `host.hooks().observe(handler)`.\n\n### 5d. phi-lua changes\n\nThis is the most substantial migration. The current `LuaExtension::register()` flow:\n\n1. Creates Lua VM, sets up `phi.on(event_name, handler)` global\n2. Loads Lua source, reads `_phi_registered_events`\n3. For each event string, calls `host.subscribe(EventHandlerRegistration { event: EventKind, handler: closure })`\n4. The closure serializes payload to JSON, sends to Lua thread, decodes return via `decode_event_return()`\n\n**New flow:**\n\n1. Same Lua VM setup (phi.on stays the same for UX)\n2. For each event string, use `host.hooks().on_*()` to register a typed handler\n3. The typed handler wraps the same Lua call mechanism but returns the typed result struct\n\nThe key challenge: Lua handlers return generic JSON objects, so `decode_event_return()` must become **per-event-type decoders**:\n\n```rust\n// phi-lua/src/extension.rs\n\nimpl Extension for LuaExtension {\n    fn register(&self, host: &mut dyn ExtensionHost) -> Result<()> {\n        // ... Lua VM setup unchanged ...\n\n        let scope = host.hooks().create_scope(SourceInfo {\n            extension_id: format!("lua:{}", stem),\n            label: Some(self.path.display().to_string()),\n        });\n\n        for event_name in event_names {\n            register_lua_hook(&scope, &event_name, Arc::clone(&tx))?;\n        }\n\n        Ok(())\n    }\n}\n\nfn register_lua_hook(\n    scope: &PhiHooksScope,\n    event_name: &str,\n    tx: Arc<SyncSender<LuaMsg>>,\n) -> Result<()> {\n    let tx = tx;\n    let name = event_name.to_owned();\n\n    match event_name {\n        "context" => {\n            scope.on::<ContextEvent, _>(move |event: &ContextEvent| {\n                let payload_json = serde_json::to_string(event)?;\n                let result_json = call_lua_handler(&tx, &name, &payload_json)?;\n                decode_context_result(result_json, event)\n            });\n        }\n        "tool_call" => {\n            scope.on::<ToolCallEvent, _>(move |event: &ToolCallEvent| {\n                let payload_json = serde_json::to_string(event)?;\n                let result_json = call_lua_handler(&tx, &name, &payload_json)?;\n                decode_tool_call_result(result_json)\n            });\n        }\n        "tool_result" => {\n            scope.on::<ToolResultEvent, _>(move |event: &ToolResultEvent| {\n                let payload_json = serde_json::to_string(event)?;\n                let result_json = call_lua_handler(&tx, &name, &payload_json)?;\n                decode_tool_result_result(result_json, event)\n            });\n        }\n        "before_agent_start" => {\n            scope.on::<BeforeAgentStartEvent, _>(move |event: &BeforeAgentStartEvent| {\n                let payload_json = serde_json::to_string(event)?;\n                let result_json = call_lua_handler(&tx, &name, &payload_json)?;\n                decode_before_agent_start_result(result_json, event)\n            });\n        }\n        "input" => {\n            scope.on::<InputEvent, _>(move |event: &InputEvent| {\n                let payload_json = serde_json::to_string(event)?;\n                let result_json = call_lua_handler(&tx, &name, &payload_json)?;\n                decode_input_result(result_json)\n            });\n        }\n        // Observation-only events\n        "runtime_start" | "session_start" | "tui_ready" | "agent_start" | "agent_end"\n        | "turn_start" | "turn_end" | "session_shutdown" => {\n            scope.on::<TurnStartEvent, _>(move |_event| {\n                let payload_json = serde_json::json!({}).to_string();  // or serialize event\n                let _ = call_lua_handler(&tx, &name, &payload_json)?;\n                Ok(None)  // VoidResult\n            });\n            // Note: need per-event-type registration here, not just TurnStartEvent\n        }\n        other => return Err(anyhow::anyhow!("unknown event: {}", other)),\n    }\n    Ok(())\n}\n```\n\nPer-event decoders replace the monolithic `decode_event_return()`:\n\n```rust\nfn decode_context_result(json_opt: Option<String>, event: &ContextEvent) -> Result<Option<ContextResult>> {\n    let Some(s) = json_opt else { return Ok(None); };\n    let v: Value = serde_json::from_str(&s)?;\n    let obj = v.as_object().ok_or_else(|| anyhow!("not an object"))?;\n\n    if let Some(msgs) = obj.get("messages").and_then(|v| v.as_array()) {\n        let messages = msgs.iter().filter_map(|m| {\n            Some(AgentMessage {\n                role: m.get("role")?.as_str()?.to_owned(),\n                content: m.get("content")?.as_str()?.to_owned(),\n            })\n        }).collect();\n        return Ok(Some(ContextResult { messages: Some(messages) }));\n    }\n    Ok(None)\n}\n\nfn decode_tool_call_result(json_opt: Option<String>) -> Result<Option<ToolCallResult>> {\n    let Some(s) = json_opt else { return Ok(None); };\n    let v: Value = serde_json::from_str(&s)?;\n    let obj = v.as_object().ok_or_else(|| anyhow!("not an object"))?;\n\n    if obj.get("block").and_then(|v| v.as_bool()).unwrap_or(false) {\n        return Ok(Some(ToolCallResult {\n            block: true,\n            reason: obj.get("reason").and_then(|v| v.as_str()).map(str::to_owned),\n        }));\n    }\n    Ok(None)\n}\n```\n\nThe `parse_event_kind()` function and `EventKind` enum are no longer needed in phi-lua -- the `match event_name` in `register_lua_hook` serves the same routing purpose.\n\n### 5e. Backward compatibility\n\nDuring migration (steps 1-5), both the old `subscribe()` and new `hooks()` coexist:\n- `AppRuntime::dispatch()` first tries `hooks.emit()` for typed events, falls back to old `event_handlers` for any remaining `EventHandlerRegistration` entries\n- Once all call sites are migrated (phi-lua fully ported), `subscribe()`, `EventKind`, `EventPayload`, `EventReturn`, `LifecycleEvent`, and `EventHandlerRegistration` are deleted (step 6)\n\n---\n\n## 6. How AppRuntime::dispatch() Evolves\n\n### Before (current)\n\n```rust\npub async fn dispatch(&self, event: LifecycleEvent) -> Result<Vec<EventReturn>> {\n    let mut returns = Vec::new();\n    for reg in self.event_handlers.iter().filter(|h| h.event == event.kind) {\n        let h = reg.handler.clone();\n        let ev = event.clone();\n        let result = tokio::task::spawn_blocking(move || h(ev)).await??;\n        if let Some(r) = result { returns.push(r); }\n    }\n    Ok(returns)\n}\n```\n\nProblems: results not chained (each handler sees original event), no typed results, no source metadata, no observe vs participate distinction, no cleanup lifecycle.\n\n### After (target)\n\n```rust\nimpl AppRuntime {\n    /// Thin delegate to PhiHooks::emit().\n    /// This replaces the old dispatch() entirely.\n    pub async fn dispatch<E: HookEvent>(&self, event: E) -> Result<Option<E::Result>> {\n        self.hooks.emit(event).await\n    }\n\n    /// System prompt injection -- now uses typed BeforeAgentStartEvent.\n    pub async fn filter_system_prompt(&self, prompt: &str, system_prompt: String) -> Result<String> {\n        let result = self.hooks.emit(BeforeAgentStartEvent {\n            prompt: prompt.to_owned(),\n            system_prompt: system_prompt.clone(),\n        }).await?;\n\n        match result {\n            Some(r) => Ok(r.system_prompt.unwrap_or(system_prompt)),\n            None => Ok(system_prompt),\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Context injection -- now uses typed ContextEvent with chaining.\n    pub async fn context_messages(&self, turn: u32, messages: Vec<AgentMessage>) -> Result<Vec<AgentMessage>> {\n        let result = self.hooks.emit(ContextEvent {\n            turn,\n            messages: messages.clone(),\n        }).await?;\n\n        match result {\n            Some(r) => Ok(r.messages.unwrap_or(messages)),\n            None => Ok(messages),\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Lifecycle helpers -- each emits the corresponding typed event.\n    pub async fn boot(&self) -> Result<()> {\n        self.hooks.emit(RuntimeStartEvent).await?;\n        Ok(())\n    }\n\n    pub async fn start_session(&self) -> Result<()> {\n        self.hooks.emit(SessionStartEvent {\n            cwd: self.cwd.display().to_string(),\n            session_id: self.session_id.clone(),\n        }).await?;\n        Ok(())\n    }\n\n    pub async fn shutdown_session(&self) -> Result<()> {\n        self.hooks.emit(SessionShutdownEvent).await?;\n        // Also run cleanups\n        self.hooks.clear().await?;\n        Ok(())\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Key differences\n\n| Aspect | Before | After |\n|--------|--------|-------|\n| Dispatch signature | `dispatch(LifecycleEvent) -> Vec<EventReturn>` | `dispatch<E: HookEvent>(E) -> Option<E::Result>` |\n| Handler visibility | Each sees original event clone | Each sees current chained state |\n| Result type | Unstructured `Vec<EventReturn>` | Typed `Option<E::Result>` |\n| Observer/participant | No distinction | `observe()` vs `on()` |\n| Source tracking | No | `SourceInfo` on every entry |\n| Cleanup | No | `add_cleanup()`, `clear()`, `dispose()` |\n| Error handling | Propagates immediately | `ErrorMode::Continue` (default) or `Throw` |\n\n### Async considerations\n\nThe current `dispatch()` wraps sync handlers in `spawn_blocking`. In the new design:\n\n- **Handlers are sync** (`Fn(&E) -> Result<Option<E::Result>>`), matching the current `EventHandler` pattern and the Lua thread channel model.\n- **`emit()` is async** because it may need to await between handler invocations (for future async handlers) and to integrate with the tokio runtime.\n- **`spawn_blocking` is used per-handler** inside `emit()` to avoid blocking the async runtime when calling sync handlers:\n\n```rust\nasync fn run_handlers_chained<E: HookEvent>(&self, event: &E) -> Result<Option<E::Result>> {\n    // ... for each handler:\n    let handler = entry.handler.clone();\n    let event_clone = event.clone();  // E: Clone\n    let result = tokio::task::spawn_blocking(move || {\n        let event_ref: &dyn Any = &event_clone;\n        handler(event_ref)\n    }).await??;\n    // ... process result\n}\n```\n\n- Future evolution: an `AsyncHandler` variant can be added later if needed (`AsyncHookHandler = Arc<dyn Fn(&E) -> BoxFuture<Result<Option<E::Result>>>`), but the initial design keeps handlers sync to match the Lua channel model and minimize complexity.\n\n### RuntimeBuilder changes\n\n```rust\nimpl RuntimeBuilder {\n    pub fn build(self) -> Result<AppRuntime> {\n        let hooks = PhiHooks::new(ErrorMode::Continue);\n        let mut host = RegistryHost {\n            tools: BTreeMap::new(),\n            commands: BTreeMap::new(),\n            hooks: hooks,  // shared reference\n            event_handlers: Vec::new(),  // deprecated\n        };\n\n        let mut manifests = Vec::new();\n        for extension in &self.extensions {\n            manifests.push(extension.manifest());\n            extension.register(&mut host)?;\n        }\n\n        Ok(AppRuntime {\n            cwd: self.cwd,\n            session_id: self.session_id,\n            extensions: manifests,\n            tools: host.tools,\n            commands: host.commands,\n            hooks: host.hooks,  // AppRuntime owns PhiHooks\n            event_handlers: host.event_handlers,  // deprecated\n            notifications: Mutex::new(Vec::new()),\n            status: Mutex::new(BTreeMap::new()),\n        })\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Snapshot evolution\n\n`RuntimeSnapshot` and `EventHandlerSummary` are updated to reflect the new hooks system. Instead of listing `EventKind` + `label`, it introspects `PhiHooks`:\n\n```rust\npub struct HookSummary {\n    pub event_type: String,    // e.g. "context", "tool_call"\n    pub source: Option<SourceInfo>,\n}\n\nimpl AppRuntime {\n    pub fn snapshot(&self) -> RuntimeSnapshot {\n        // ... other fields ...\n        let hook_summaries: Vec<HookSummary> = self.hooks.handler_summaries();\n        // ...\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nThe Phase 3 hooks refactor replaces Phi\'s untyped, un-chained event enum system with a typed, chained hooks system. The key Rust translation decisions are:\n\n1. **Associated types** on a `HookEvent` trait replace TypeScript\'s phantom type parameters.\n2. **`TypeId` + `Any` downcast** replaces TypeScript\'s string-keyed `Map<string, ...>` for type-erased handler storage.\n3. **Per-event-type reducers** (matched on `TypeId` inside `emit()`) encode the chaining semantics: context transform, tool_call early-exit, tool_result patch accumulation, etc.\n4. **`PhiHooksScope`** carries `SourceInfo` for attribution.\n5. **Migration is incremental**: old `subscribe()` coexists with new `hooks()` until all call sites (primarily phi-lua) are ported.\n6. **`dispatch()` becomes a thin generic delegate**: `dispatch<E: HookEvent>(E) -> Option<E::Result>`, with typed helper methods replacing the old string-payload dispatchers.\n"""\n\nwrite_file("/tmp/phase3_plan.md", plan_text)\nprint(f"Plan written: {len(plan_text)} chars")\nprint("\\n=== First 500 chars ===")\nprint(plan_text[:500])\n```', '# PHASE 4: DURABILITY + OBSERVABILITY — Implementation Plan\n\n## 1. New Durable Entry Types\n\nPhi extends the existing JSONL session schema with operation-level durable entries. Each entry is a single JSON object per line, tagged with a `type` discriminator field for serde internally-tagged representation.\n\n### 1.1 Enum: SessionEntry\n\nAll existing and new entry types are unified under one internally-tagged enum:\n\n```rust\n#[serde(tag = "type")]\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub enum SessionEntry {\n    // Existing entries\n    Session(SessionHeader),\n    Message(MessageEntry),\n    Repl(ReplEntry),\n    ExchangeStart(ExchangeStartEntry),\n    ExchangeEnd(ExchangeEndEntry),\n    Leaf(LeafEntry),\n    // New durable entries\n    OperationStart(OperationStartEntry),\n    OperationFinish(OperationFinishEntry),\n    OperationInterrupted(OperationInterruptedEntry),\n    TurnStart(TurnStartEntry),\n    TurnFinish(TurnFinishEntry),\n    ToolCallStart(ToolCallStartEntry),\n    ToolCallFinish(ToolCallFinishEntry),\n    QueueEnqueued(QueueEnqueuedEntry),\n    QueueConsumed(QueueConsumedEntry),\n    PendingWriteEnqueued(PendingWriteEnqueuedEntry),\n    PendingWriteApplied(PendingWriteAppliedEntry),\n}\n```\n\n### 1.2 Operation Entries\n\nOperations are coarse-grained units of work: compaction, tree ops, provider calls, tool calls. Each has a unique `op_id`, a kind, an optional parent for nesting, and a trace/span for observability correlation.\n\n```rust\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct OperationStartEntry {\n    pub op_id: Uuid,\n    pub op_kind: OperationKind,\n    pub parent_op_id: Option<Uuid>,\n    pub trace_id: TraceId,    // newtype wrapper around String\n    pub span_id: SpanId,      // newtype wrapper around String\n    pub started_at: DateTime<Utc>,\n    pub payload: SafePayload, // already-redacted\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct OperationFinishEntry {\n    pub op_id: Uuid,\n    pub finished_at: DateTime<Utc>,\n    pub duration_ms: u64,\n    pub result_summary: Option<String>,\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct OperationInterruptedEntry {\n    pub op_id: Uuid,\n    pub interrupted_at: DateTime<Utc>,\n    pub reason: InterruptReason,\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n#[serde(rename_all = "snake_case")]\npub enum OperationKind {\n    Compaction,\n    TreeOp,\n    ProviderRequest,\n    ToolCall,\n    Turn,\n    Prompt,\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n#[serde(rename_all = "snake_case")]\npub enum InterruptReason {\n    Crash,\n    Timeout,\n    UserAbort,\n    DependencyError,\n    RecoveryMarked,\n}\n```\n### 1.3 Turn Entries\n\nTurns are the top-level agent loop iteration. `turn_start` records which queue items were consumed, linking durable queue entries to the turn that processed them.\n\n```rust\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct TurnStartEntry {\n    pub turn_id: Uuid,\n    pub trace_id: TraceId,\n    pub span_id: SpanId,\n    pub started_at: DateTime<Utc>,\n    pub consumed_queue_ids: Vec<Uuid>,  // links to QueueEnqueued entries\n    pub op_id: Uuid,  // parent operation for this turn\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct TurnFinishEntry {\n    pub turn_id: Uuid,\n    pub finished_at: DateTime<Utc>,\n    pub duration_ms: u64,\n    pub stop_reason: Option<String>,\n}\n```\n\n### 1.4 Tool Call Entries\n\nTool calls are instrumented at the boundary: start records the tool name and a deterministic call ID; finish records duration and a safe status. Tool arguments and results are NOT stored (unsafe).\n\n```rust\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct ToolCallStartEntry {\n    pub call_id: Uuid,\n    pub tool_name: String,\n    pub op_id: Uuid,      // parent operation\n    pub turn_id: Uuid,    // parent turn\n    pub trace_id: TraceId,\n    pub span_id: SpanId,\n    pub started_at: DateTime<Utc>,\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct ToolCallFinishEntry {\n    pub call_id: Uuid,\n    pub finished_at: DateTime<Utc>,\n    pub duration_ms: u64,\n    pub success: bool,\n    pub error_type: Option<String>,  // safe: error category only, not message\n}\n```\n\n### 1.5 Queue Entries\n\nThe durable queue enables crash-safe work item processing. Items are enqueued before work begins and marked consumed when a turn starts processing them.\n\n```rust\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct QueueEnqueuedEntry {\n    pub queue_id: Uuid,\n    pub enqueued_at: DateTime<Utc>,\n    pub item_kind: QueueItemKind,\n    pub item_ref: Option<String>,  // safe reference (e.g. message_id), not content\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct QueueConsumedEntry {\n    pub queue_id: Uuid,\n    pub consumed_at: DateTime<Utc>,\n    pub turn_id: Uuid,  // which turn consumed this\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n#[serde(rename_all = "snake_case")]\npub enum QueueItemKind {\n    UserMessage,\n    CompactionTask,\n    TreeOperation,\n    SystemTask,\n}\n```\n\n### 1.6 Pending Write Entries\n\nPending writes buffer file mutations with deterministic target IDs so they can be applied idempotently during recovery. The target ID is a hash of the target path + content hash, ensuring re-application is safe.\n\n```rust\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct PendingWriteEnqueuedEntry {\n    pub write_id: Uuid,\n    pub target_id: String,  // deterministic: blake3(path + content_hash)\n    pub target_path: String,\n    pub enqueued_at: DateTime<Utc>,\n    pub op_id: Uuid,  // parent operation\n    pub content_hash: String,  // blake3 hex\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct PendingWriteAppliedEntry {\n    pub write_id: Uuid,\n    pub applied_at: DateTime<Utc>,\n    pub target_id: String,  // matches enqueued target_id\n    pub success: bool,\n}\n```\n\n## 2. Recovery Logic\n\n### 2.1 Recovery State\n\n`PhiHarness::recover()` reduces the JSONL log by folding every entry into a `RecoveryState` struct. This struct accumulates the known state of the session after replay.\n\n```rust\n#[derive(Debug, Default)]\npub struct RecoveryState {\n    pub session_header: Option<SessionHeader>,\n    pub messages: Vec<MessageEntry>,      // DAG nodes\n    pub current_leaf: Option<LeafEntry>,\n    pub operations: HashMap<Uuid, OperationState>,\n    pub turns: HashMap<Uuid, TurnState>,\n    pub tool_calls: HashMap<Uuid, ToolCallState>,\n    pub queue: HashMap<Uuid, QueueItemState>,\n    pub pending_writes: HashMap<Uuid, PendingWriteState>,\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Default)]\npub struct OperationState {\n    pub kind: Option<OperationKind>,\n    pub status: OpStatus,\n    pub parent_op_id: Option<Uuid>,\n    pub trace_id: Option<TraceId>,\n    pub span_id: Option<SpanId>,\n    pub started_at: Option<DateTime<Utc>>,\n    pub finished_at: Option<DateTime<Utc>>,\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Default, PartialEq)]\n#[serde(rename_all = "snake_case")]\npub enum OpStatus {\n    #[default]\n    Unknown,    // started entry not found\n    Started,\n    Finished,\n    Interrupted,\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Default)]\npub struct TurnState {\n    pub started_at: Option<DateTime<Utc>>,\n    pub finished_at: Option<DateTime<Utc>>,\n    pub consumed_queue_ids: Vec<Uuid>,\n    pub status: OpStatus,\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Default)]\npub struct ToolCallState {\n    pub tool_name: Option<String>,\n    pub status: OpStatus,\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Default)]\npub struct QueueItemState {\n    pub kind: Option<QueueItemKind>,\n    pub enqueued: bool,\n    pub consumed: bool,\n    pub consumed_by_turn: Option<Uuid>,\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Default)]\npub struct PendingWriteState {\n    pub target_path: String,\n    pub target_id: String,\n    pub content_hash: String,\n    pub applied: bool,\n}\n```\n\n### 2.2 Log Reduction Algorithm\n\nRecovery is a left fold over the JSONL log:\n\n```rust\nimpl PhiHarness {\n    pub fn recover(path: &Path) -> Result<RecoveryState, RecoveryError> {\n        let reader = BufReader::new(File::open(path)?);\n        let mut state = RecoveryState::default();\n\n        for (line_num, line) in reader.lines().enumerate() {\n            let line = line.map_err(|e| RecoveryError::IO { line: line_num, source: e })?;\n            if line.trim().is_empty() { continue; }\n            let entry: SessionEntry = serde_json::from_str(&line)\n                .map_err(|e| RecoveryError::Parse { line: line_num, source: e })?;\n            Self::apply_entry(&mut state, entry, line_num)?;\n        }\n\n        // Post-pass: reconcile unfinished operations\n        Self::reconcile(&mut state);\n        Ok(state)\n    }\n\n    fn apply_entry(state: &mut RecoveryState, entry: SessionEntry, line: usize) -> Result<(), RecoveryError> {\n        match entry {\n            SessionEntry::Session(h) => state.session_header = Some(h),\n            SessionEntry::Message(m) => state.messages.push(m),\n            SessionEntry::Leaf(l) => state.current_leaf = Some(l),\n            SessionEntry::OperationStart(e) => {\n                state.operations.insert(e.op_id, OperationState {\n                    kind: Some(e.op_kind),\n                    status: OpStatus::Started,\n                    parent_op_id: e.parent_op_id,\n                    trace_id: Some(e.trace_id),\n                    span_id: Some(e.span_id),\n                    started_at: Some(e.started_at),\n                    finished_at: None,\n                });\n            }\n            SessionEntry::OperationFinish(e) => {\n                if let Some(op) = state.operations.get_mut(&e.op_id) {\n                    op.status = OpStatus::Finished;\n                    op.finished_at = Some(e.finished_at);\n                }\n            }\n            SessionEntry::TurnStart(e) => {\n                state.turns.insert(e.turn_id, TurnState {\n                    started_at: Some(e.started_at),\n                    finished_at: None,\n                    consumed_queue_ids: e.consumed_queue_ids.clone(),\n                    status: OpStatus::Started,\n                });\n                // Mark consumed queue items\n                for qid in &e.consumed_queue_ids {\n                    if let Some(q) = state.queue.get_mut(qid) {\n                        q.consumed = true;\n                        q.consumed_by_turn = Some(e.turn_id);\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n            SessionEntry::TurnFinish(e) => {\n                if let Some(t) = state.turns.get_mut(&e.turn_id) {\n                    t.status = OpStatus::Finished;\n                    t.finished_at = Some(e.finished_at);\n                }\n            }\n            // ... ToolCall, Queue, PendingWrite entries applied similarly\n            _ => {} // existing entries (ExchangeStart/End, Repl) handled as before\n        }\n        Ok(())\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### 2.3 Post-Pass Reconciliation\n\nAfter the fold, `reconcile()` walks all operations/turns/tool_calls and applies the conservative interruption policy:\n\n```rust\nimpl PhiHarness {\n    fn reconcile(state: &mut RecoveryState) {\n        // Any operation still in Started status is unfinished -> mark Interrupted\n        for (op_id, op) in &mut state.operations {\n            if op.status == OpStatus::Started {\n                op.status = OpStatus::Interrupted;\n            }\n        }\n        // Any turn still Started -> mark Interrupted\n        for (turn_id, turn) in &mut state.turns {\n            if turn.status == OpStatus::Started {\n                turn.status = OpStatus::Interrupted;\n            }\n        }\n        // Pending writes: if not applied, re-enqueue for application\n        // (deterministic target_id makes re-application idempotent)\n        // Tool calls: do NOT retry unless retry-safe. Interrupted tool calls\n        // are marked Interrupted and left for the user/agent to decide.\n        // Compaction/tree ops: only rerun if no OperationFinish exists.\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Conservative interruption policy summary:**\n\n| Entry Type | Unfinished State | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| Operation | Started (no finish/interrupted) | Mark `Interrupted`. Rerun compaction/tree ops only if no final entry exists. |\n| Turn | Started (no turn_finish) | Mark `Interrupted`. Re-enqueue consumed queue items. |\n| Tool Call | Started (no tool_call_finish) | Mark `Interrupted`. Do NOT retry unless retry-safe (idempotent tool + no observed side effects). |\n| Queue | Enqueued, not consumed | Leave in queue; available for next turn. |\n| Pending Write | Enqueued, not applied | Re-enqueue for application. Idempotent via `target_id`. |\n\n### 2.4 Runtime Dependency Validation\n\nDuring recovery, `PhiHarness` validates that runtime dependencies are available before resuming:\n\n```rust\npub fn validate_runtime_deps(state: &RecoveryState) -> Result<(), RecoveryError> {\n    // 1. Check provider API keys are set (env or config)\n    // 2. Check working directory exists and is accessible\n    // 3. Check session log is writable (for appending recovery markers)\n    // 4. Verify any pending_write target directories exist\n    Ok(())\n}\n```\n\n## 3. ObservabilityContext and ObservabilityEvent\n\n### 3.1 Design Decision: tokio::task_local!\n\nPhi uses `tokio::task_local!` for context propagation, not explicit threading. Rationale:\n\n- Phi\'s engine is fully async under tokio. Every operation runs inside a tokio task.\n- Explicit context threading would require adding a `ctx: &ObservabilityContext` parameter to dozens of functions, polluting the call graph and risking dropped context at boundaries.\n- `task_local!` provides automatic propagation across `.await` points within the same task, with `Send + Sync` guarantees from tokio\'s runtime.\n- The main drawback — context is lost when spawning a new task — is handled by explicitly passing the context into child spawns (see 3.4).\n\n### 3.2 Struct Definitions\n\n```rust\nuse std::sync::Arc;\nuse tokio::task_local;\n\n// Newtype wrappers for type safety\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Hash, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct TraceId(pub String);  // e.g. "trace-<uuid>"\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Hash, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct SpanId(pub String);  // e.g. "span-<uuid>"\n\n// Task-local observability context\ntask_local! {\n    pub static OBS_CONTEXT: Arc<ObservabilityContext>;\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone)]\npub struct ObservabilityContext {\n    pub trace_id: TraceId,\n    pub current_span_id: SpanId,\n    pub user_context: serde_json::Value,  // arbitrary safe metadata\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone)]\npub struct ObservabilityEvent {\n    pub event_type: EventType,  // "start" | "finish" | "error"\n    pub name: String,           // stable event name, e.g. "phi.agent.turn"\n    pub trace_id: TraceId,\n    pub span_id: SpanId,\n    pub parent_span_id: Option<SpanId>,\n    pub timestamp: DateTime<Utc>,\n    pub duration_ms: Option<u64>,\n    pub context: serde_json::Value,  // safe context metadata\n    pub payload: SafePayload,\n    pub error: Option<String>,  // safe error category, not full message\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n#[serde(rename_all = "snake_case")]\npub enum EventType {\n    Start,\n    Finish,\n    Error,\n}\n```\n\n### 3.3 trace_operation Function\n\n`trace_operation` is the primary instrumentation entry point. It mirrors Pi\'s `traceOperation`: read the current context, create a child span, emit a start event, run the callback under the child context, emit a finish/error event.\n\n```rust\npub async fn trace_operation<F, T, E>(\n    name: &str,\n    payload: SafePayload,\n    subscriber: &dyn ObservabilitySubscriber,\n    f: F,\n) -> Result<T, E>\nwhere\n    F: std::future::Future<Output = Result<T, E>>,\n    E: std::fmt::Display,\n{\n    let parent_ctx = OBS_CONTEXT.try_with(|ctx| (*ctx).clone())\n        .unwrap_or_else(|_| Arc::new(ObservabilityContext {\n            trace_id: TraceId(uuid::Uuid::new_v4().to_string()),\n            current_span_id: SpanId(uuid::Uuid::new_v4().to_string()),\n            user_context: serde_json::Value::Null,\n        }));\n\n    let child_span_id = SpanId(uuid::Uuid::new_v4().to_string());\n    let child_ctx = Arc::new(ObservabilityContext {\n        trace_id: parent_ctx.trace_id.clone(),\n        current_span_id: child_span_id.clone(),\n        user_context: parent_ctx.user_context.clone(),\n    });\n\n    let start_event = ObservabilityEvent {\n        event_type: EventType::Start,\n        name: name.to_string(),\n        trace_id: child_ctx.trace_id.clone(),\n        span_id: child_span_id.clone(),\n        parent_span_id: Some(parent_ctx.current_span_id.clone()),\n        timestamp: Utc::now(),\n        duration_ms: None,\n        context: child_ctx.user_context.clone(),\n        payload: payload.clone(),\n        error: None,\n    };\n    subscriber.emit(start_event);\n\n    let start_instant = std::time::Instant::now();\n    let result = OBS_CONTEXT.scope(child_ctx, f).await;\n    let duration_ms = start_instant.elapsed().as_millis() as u64;\n\n    match &result {\n        Ok(_) => {\n            let finish_event = ObservabilityEvent {\n                event_type: EventType::Finish,\n                name: name.to_string(),\n                trace_id: child_ctx.trace_id.clone(), // from parent capture\n                span_id: child_span_id,\n                parent_span_id: Some(parent_ctx.current_span_id.clone()),\n                timestamp: Utc::now(),\n                duration_ms: Some(duration_ms),\n                context: parent_ctx.user_context.clone(),\n                payload,\n                error: None,\n            };\n            subscriber.emit(finish_event);\n        }\n        Err(e) => {\n            let error_event = ObservabilityEvent {\n                event_type: EventType::Error,\n                name: name.to_string(),\n                trace_id: parent_ctx.trace_id.clone(),\n                span_id: child_span_id,\n                parent_span_id: Some(parent_ctx.current_span_id.clone()),\n                timestamp: Utc::now(),\n                duration_ms: Some(duration_ms),\n                context: parent_ctx.user_context.clone(),\n                payload,\n                error: Some(format!("{}", e)), // safe: error category via Display\n            };\n            subscriber.emit(error_event);\n        }\n    }\n    result\n}\n```\n\n### 3.4 Spawning Child Tasks\n\nWhen spawning a new tokio task (e.g. a batched sub-call), the context must be explicitly passed, since `task_local!` does not cross `tokio::spawn` boundaries:\n\n```rust\nlet parent_ctx = OBS_CONTEXT.try_with(|c| (*c).clone()).unwrap_or_default();\ntokio::spawn(async move {\n    OBS_CONTEXT.scope(parent_ctx, async {\n        // traced work here\n    }).await\n});\n```\n\n## 4. Stable Event Names and Instrumentation Points\n\nEvent names follow the `phi.<area>.<action>` convention. Each is emitted at a specific point in the engine:\n\n| Event Name | Instrumentation Point | Payload (SafePayload) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `phi.agent.prompt` | `PhiEngine::build_prompt()` — constructs the prompt for a provider call | `{ session_id, message_count }` |\n| `phi.agent.turn` | `PhiEngine::run_turn()` — one iteration of the agent loop | `{ session_id, turn_id, consumed_queue_ids_count }` |\n| `phi.ai.provider.request` | `PhiEngine::provider_call()` — sends a request to the LLM provider | `{ provider, model, api, session_id }` + finish: `{ stop_reason, token_counts, cost }` |\n| `phi.agent.tool_call` | `PhiEngine::execute_tool()` — dispatches a tool (REPL, bash, file ops) | `{ tool_name, call_id }` + finish: `{ success, duration_ms }` |\n| `phi.agent.repl` | `PhiEngine::execute_repl()` — executes a REPL code block | `{ block_id, tools_used }` + finish: `{ success, duration_ms }` |\n| `phi.agent.exchange` | `PhiEngine::run_exchange()` — wraps a user-to-assistant exchange | `{ session_id, exchange_id }` |\n| `phi.operation.compaction` | `PhiHarness::run_compaction()` — context window compaction | `{ strategy, messages_before, messages_after }` |\n| `phi.operation.tree` | `PhiHarness::run_tree_op()` — branch/merge tree operation | `{ op_kind, node_count }` |\n| `phi.queue.enqueue` | `PhiQueue::enqueue()` — adds an item to the durable queue | `{ queue_id, item_kind }` |\n| `phi.queue.consume` | `PhiQueue::consume()` — marks an item consumed by a turn | `{ queue_id, turn_id }` |\n| `phi.pending_write.enqueue` | `PhiHarness::enqueue_write()` — buffers a file write | `{ write_id, target_id, target_path }` |\n| `phi.pending_write.apply` | `PhiHarness::apply_write()` — applies a buffered write to disk | `{ write_id, target_id, success }` |\n\n### 4.1 Instrumentation Example\n\n```rust\nimpl PhiEngine {\n    async fn run_turn(&self, turn_id: Uuid, queue_ids: Vec<Uuid>) -> Result<TurnResult, PhiError> {\n        let payload = SafePayload::json(serde_json::json!({\n            "session_id": self.session_id,\n            "turn_id": turn_id,\n            "consumed_queue_ids_count": queue_ids.len(),\n        }));\n        trace_operation("phi.agent.turn", payload, self.subscriber.as_ref(), async {\n            // ... actual turn logic ...\n            // Also append TurnStart/TurnFinish durable entries\n        }).await\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## 5. Safe Payload Redaction\n\n### 5.1 Safe vs Unsafe Classification\n\n| Category | Safe (included) | Unsafe (redacted) |\n|---|---|---|\n| LLM interaction | provider, model, api endpoint, stop\\_reason, token\\_counts, cost | prompts, completions, system messages |\n| Tool execution | tool\\_name, call\\_id, success, duration\\_ms, error\\_type | tool args, tool results, shell stdout/stderr, file contents |\n| Session | session\\_id, turn\\_id, message count, leaf node id | message content, user text, assistant text |\n| Infrastructure | api (name only, not key), queue\\_id, write\\_id, target\\_path, content\\_hash | api keys, secrets, env var values |\n| Counts | token counts, cost, durations, message counts | (all counts are safe) |\n\n### 5.2 Redaction Mechanism: SafePayload Type\n\nPhi uses a `SafePayload` newtype that enforces redaction at construction time. It is impossible to accidentally embed unsafe data — the only constructor takes explicitly-typed safe fields.\n\n```rust\n/// A payload that has been vetted as safe for external observability consumers.\n/// Cannot be constructed from arbitrary JSON — must go through SafePayload::json()\n/// which validates against a redaction schema, or SafePayload::empty().\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct SafePayload {\n    fields: serde_json::Map<String, serde_json::Value>,\n}\n\nimpl SafePayload {\n    /// Construct from a JSON value, applying field-level redaction.\n    /// Any field matching an unsafe key pattern is replaced with "<redacted>".\n    pub fn json(mut value: serde_json::Value) -> Self {\n        Self::redact_in_place(&mut value);\n        let fields = value.take().as_object().cloned().unwrap_or_default();\n        SafePayload { fields }\n    }\n\n    pub fn empty() -> Self {\n        SafePayload { fields: serde_json::Map::new() }\n    }\n\n    /// Recursive redaction: walks the JSON tree and replaces values\n    /// for keys matching the unsafe pattern set.\n    fn redact_in_place(value: &mut serde_json::Value) {\n        match value {\n            serde_json::Value::Object(map) => {\n                for (key, val) in map.iter_mut() {\n                    if Self::is_unsafe_key(key) {\n                        *val = serde_json::Value::String("<redacted>".to_string());\n                    } else {\n                        Self::redact_in_place(val);\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n            serde_json::Value::Array(arr) => {\n                for item in arr {\n                    Self::redact_in_place(item);\n                }\n            }\n            _ => {}\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Keys (case-insensitive substring match) that indicate unsafe data.\n    fn is_unsafe_key(key: &str) -> bool {\n        let k = key.to_lowercase();\n        const UNSAFE_PATTERNS: &[&str] = &[\n            "prompt", "completion", "content", "message_text", "text",\n            "args", "arguments", "result", "output", "stdout", "stderr",\n            "file_content", "body", "secret", "api_key", "token", "password",\n        ];\n        UNSAFE_PATTERNS.iter().any(|p| k.contains(p))\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Key insight**: `SafePayload::is_unsafe_key` uses deny-list matching for defense in depth, but the primary guarantee is that instrumented call sites construct payloads from explicitly safe fields (counts, IDs, durations, enums). The redaction pass is a safety net, not the sole mechanism.\n\n## 6. Subscriber API\n\n### 6.1 Subscriber Trait\n\nExternal consumers implement the `ObservabilitySubscriber` trait and register with the engine. Events are emitted synchronously by default; async/channel-based subscribers handle their own buffering.\n\n```rust\n/// A consumer of observability events. Implementations may forward to\n/// OTLP, a file, a TUI, or any external system.\npub trait ObservabilitySubscriber: Send + Sync {\n    fn emit(&self, event: ObservabilityEvent);\n}\n\n/// A subscriber that does nothing (default when observability is off).\npub struct NoopSubscriber;\nimpl ObservabilitySubscriber for NoopSubscriber {\n    fn emit(&self, _event: ObservabilityEvent) {}\n}\n\n/// A subscriber that writes events as JSONL to a file.\npub struct JsonlFileSubscriber {\n    writer: Mutex<BufWriter<File>>,\n}\nimpl ObservabilitySubscriber for JsonlFileSubscriber {\n    fn emit(&self, event: ObservabilityEvent) {\n        let line = serde_json::to_string(&event).unwrap_or_default();\n        let mut writer = self.writer.lock().unwrap();\n        let _ = writeln!(writer, "{line}");\n        let _ = writer.flush();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### 6.2 Registration\n\nThe engine holds an `Arc<dyn ObservabilitySubscriber>`. Multiple subscribers can be composed via a fan-out wrapper:\n\n```rust\n/// Fans out events to multiple subscribers.\npub struct FanOutSubscriber {\n    subscribers: Vec<Arc<dyn ObservabilitySubscriber>>,\n}\nimpl ObservabilitySubscriber for FanOutSubscriber {\n    fn emit(&self, event: ObservabilityEvent) {\n        for s in &self.subscribers {\n            s.emit(event.clone());\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl PhiEngine {\n    pub fn with_subscriber(mut self, subscriber: Arc<dyn ObservabilitySubscriber>) -> Self {\n        self.subscriber = subscriber;\n        self\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### 6.3 Async Channel Subscriber (Backpressure)\n\nFor high-throughput or non-blocking consumers, a channel-based subscriber decouples emission from processing:\n\n```rust\npub struct ChannelSubscriber {\n    tx: tokio::sync::mpsc::Sender<ObservabilityEvent>,\n}\nimpl ObservabilitySubscriber for ChannelSubscriber {\n    fn emit(&self, event: ObservabilityEvent) {\n        // try_send: non-blocking. If the channel is full, the event is dropped\n        // to prevent observability from blocking the agent loop.\n        let _ = self.tx.try_send(event);\n    }\n}\n\n/// Construct a channel subscriber and return the receiver for the consumer.\npub fn channel_subscriber(\n    buffer: usize,\n) -> (ChannelSubscriber, tokio::sync::mpsc::Receiver<ObservabilityEvent>) {\n    let (tx, rx) = tokio::sync::mpsc::channel(buffer);\n    (ChannelSubscriber { tx }, rx)\n}\n```\n\n**Backpressure policy**: `try_send` is used (non-blocking). If the channel buffer is full, events are dropped. This ensures observability never blocks the agent\'s critical path. Dropped event counts can be tracked via a `DroppingSubscriber` wrapper that increments an `AtomicU64` counter on drop.\n\n### 6.4 TUI Integration\n\nThe existing `RlmEvent` enum for TUI consumption is preserved. A bridge subscriber converts `ObservabilityEvent`s into `RlmEvent`s for the TUI:\n\n```rust\npub struct TuiBridgeSubscriber {\n    tui_tx: tokio::sync::mpsc::Sender<RlmEvent>,\n}\nimpl ObservabilitySubscriber for TuiBridgeSubscriber {\n    fn emit(&self, event: ObservabilityEvent) {\n        if let Some(tui_event) = RlmEvent::from_observability(&event) {\n            let _ = self.tui_tx.try_send(tui_event);\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n']